# Shared publication annotations

Some explanations need a little more than the main flow of prose. A term may need
a reusable definition, while a note may deserve space beside the page instead of
interrupting the paragraph. Literate records both forms as annotations in the
book and lets each renderer decide how best to present them.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Prose[Prose and annotation fences] --> Collect[Collect terms, notes, and uses]
    Collect --> Resolve[Resolve stable identities and references]
    Resolve --> Shared[Renderer-neutral annotation model]
    Shared --> Screen[Preview and generated HTML]
    Shared --> Print[LaTeX notes and indexes]
```

The shared model deliberately stops before presentation. It says which terms and
notes exist, where they came from, and how references resolve; it does not decide
whether a note becomes a side rail, a panel, a link, or an index entry. That
separation lets screen and print disagree about appearance without disagreeing
about meaning.

Annotations are prose-local fenced blocks, not contributions to generated program
fragments. A `TERM` header names a reusable concept and gives it a display title;
a `NOTE` header takes no arguments. TERM bodies remain part of the source prose,
while NOTE bodies are available to the publication rail. Headers are
case-sensitive, and Markdown-it determines their fence boundaries, including the
behavior of an unclosed fence.

The exact accepted grammar is intentionally narrow. A term header has the form
`TERM key "Display title"`. Keys begin with an ASCII letter and may continue with
letters, digits, `_`, `.`, `:`, or `-`, up to one hundred characters. The title is
a required JSON string of at most two hundred Unicode code points. These limits
make identities predictable before any renderer sees them. The mechanical root
that assembles the annotation module is in the
[program assembly appendix](assembly.html).

## Renderer-neutral contract

Locations use zero-based source lines and UTF-16 columns. Document uses point to
the enclosing inline block; body uses point to the enclosing annotation fence.
A use's tokenIndex selects an inline token in the document tokens or in
`parseAnnotationBody(owner.body)`; childIndex selects that token's link_open child.
This intentionally does not claim character-exact Markdown link locations.
Definition tokenIndex selects the original fence. Owners are document paths for
ordinary prose and annotation targets for body prose. Lists and headings in
ordinary prose are included. Inline code, code blocks, and images are not uses.

```ts : <<annotation contracts>>=
import MarkdownIt = require('markdown-it');
import Token = require('markdown-it/lib/token');
import type { GenerationDiagnostic as Diagnostic } from './engine';

type Location = { path: string; line: number; column: number };
export interface PublicationTerm {
    key: string; title: string; target: string; at: Location; tokenIndex: number; body: string;
}
export interface PublicationNote {
    target: string; number: number; at: Location; tokenIndex: number; body: string;
}
export interface PublicationTermUse {
    key: string; target?: string; at: Location; owner: string;
    tokenIndex: number; childIndex: number;
    resolution: 'resolved' | 'missing' | 'ambiguous';
}
export interface PublicationAnnotations {
    terms: PublicationTerm[]; notes: PublicationNote[];
    uses: PublicationTermUse[]; diagnostics: Diagnostic[];
}
```

## One deterministic body parser

Bodies allow paragraphs, ordered and unordered lists, emphasis, strong emphasis,
inline code, links, text and line breaks. All other token kinds are rejected,
including headings, blockquotes, tables, strikethrough, images, indented code,
code fences (including nested TERM/NOTE and Mermaid), and raw HTML. HTML parsing
is enabled only to recognize and diagnose HTML, not to authorize rendering it.
Escaped HTML and HTML inside inline code remain ordinary text/code. The parser
returns tokens even for invalid bodies; the collector validates those same tokens.
Adapters must honor diagnostics and never render rejected HTML unsanitized.

```ts : <<annotation body parsing>>=
export function parseAnnotationBody(body: string): Token[] {
    return new MarkdownIt({ html: true }).parse(body, {});
}
const allowedBodyTokens = new Set([
    'paragraph_open', 'paragraph_close', 'inline', 'text', 'softbreak', 'hardbreak',
    'bullet_list_open', 'bullet_list_close', 'ordered_list_open', 'ordered_list_close',
    'list_item_open', 'list_item_close', 'em_open', 'em_close', 'strong_open',
    'strong_close', 'code_inline', 'link_open', 'link_close'
]);
function invalidBodyToken(tokens: readonly Token[]): Token | undefined {
    for (const token of tokens) {
        if (!allowedBodyTokens.has(token.type)) { return token; }
        const invalid = invalidBodyToken(token.children || []);
        if (invalid) { return invalid; }
    }
    return undefined;
}
```

## Stable identities and safe lookup

Targets have distinct generated namespaces and hex-encoded UTF-16 components,
never author-controlled raw identifiers. TERM targets derive from the key;
duplicate definitions receive location suffixes so preview targets remain unique.
NOTE targets derive from path and fence line/column, not the encounter number.
Numbers are one-based in caller document/fence order. Keys are case-sensitive.
Percent-encoded term destinations are decoded exactly once; malformed encodings
and invalid keys cannot resolve. Missing and ambiguous links return undefined,
never the original href. termByTarget likewise returns undefined for unknown IDs.

```ts : <<annotation identities and hrefs>>=
const keyPattern = /^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{0,99}$/;
function encode(value: string): string {
    return value.split('').map(char => char.charCodeAt(0).toString(16).padStart(4, '0')).join('');
}
function termKey(href: string): string | undefined {
    if (!href.startsWith('term:')) { return undefined; }
    try { return decodeURIComponent(href.slice(5)); } catch { return undefined; }
}
export function resolveTermHref(annotations: PublicationAnnotations, href: string): string | undefined {
    const key = termKey(href);
    if (key === undefined || !keyPattern.test(key)) { return undefined; }
    const terms = annotations.terms.filter(term => term.key === key);
    return terms.length === 1 ? terms[0].target : undefined;
}
export function termByTarget(annotations: PublicationAnnotations, target: string): PublicationTerm | undefined {
    return annotations.terms.find(term => term.target === target);
}
```

## Collection before resolution

Collection is pure with respect to caller tokens. Invalid headers produce no
annotation; valid headers retain their bodies even when body validation fails.
Every duplicate definition is diagnosed and all uses of that key are ambiguous.
Forward and cross-document uses resolve after collection. Unknown or malformed
term destinations are missing uses and diagnostic, so strict adapters can reject
them while previews retain occurrence metadata. Diagnostics have stable codes.

```ts : <<annotation collection>>=
export function collectAnnotations(
    documents: readonly { path: string; text: string; tokens: readonly Token[] }[]
): PublicationAnnotations {
    const result: PublicationAnnotations = { terms: [], notes: [], uses: [], diagnostics: [] };
    const report = (at: Location, code: string, message: string): void => {
        result.diagnostics.push({ ...at, code, message });
    };
    <<annotation inline collection>>
    <<annotation fence collection>>
    <<annotation final resolution>>
    return result;
}
```

Markdown-it emits list bodies as a flat block-token stream with inline children.
Iterating every inline token handles arbitrary list nesting without inventing
indexes or scanning raw Markdown. Image alt children are deliberately excluded.

```ts : <<annotation inline collection>>=
    const collectUses = (tokens: readonly Token[], owner: string,
        location: (token: Token) => Location, tokenOffset = 0): void => {
        tokens.forEach((token, tokenIndex) => {
            if (token.type !== 'inline') { return; }
            (token.children || []).forEach((child, childIndex) => {
                const href = child.type === 'link_open' ? child.attrGet('href') : null;
                if (!href?.startsWith('term:')) { return; }
                result.uses.push({ key: termKey(href) ?? href.slice(5), at: location(token),
                    owner, tokenIndex: tokenIndex + tokenOffset, childIndex, resolution: 'missing' });
            });
        });
    };
```

Fence recognition reserves only the exact uppercase TERM and NOTE words followed
by whitespace or end-of-header. Ordinary language fences are unchanged, including
TERM-like language names. The original Markdown-normalized fence content is body.

```ts : <<annotation fence collection>>=
    for (const document of documents) {
        const lines = document.text.split(/\r\n|\r|\n/);
        const location = (token: Token): Location => ({ path: document.path,
            line: token.map?.[0] ?? 0, column: token.type === 'fence'
                ? Math.max(0, (lines[token.map?.[0] ?? 0] || '').indexOf(token.markup)) : 0 });
        document.tokens.forEach((token, tokenIndex) => {
            if (token.type === 'inline') {
                // Preserve source encounter order with body uses between prose uses.
                collectUses([token], document.path, location, tokenIndex);
            }
            if (token.type !== 'fence' || !/^(TERM|NOTE)(?:\s|$)/.test(token.info.trim())) { return; }
            const header = token.info.trim();
            const at = location(token);
            const body = token.content;
            let annotation: PublicationTerm | PublicationNote;
            <<annotation header acceptance>>
            const bodyTokens = parseAnnotationBody(body);
            const invalid = invalidBodyToken(bodyTokens);
            if (invalid) { report(at, 'annotation-body', `Unsupported annotation body token: ${invalid.type}`); }
            collectUses(bodyTokens, annotation.target, () => at);
        });
    }
```

Required titles are parsed as JSON, rather than unescaping with a second grammar.
Duplicate target suffixes are assigned after all definitions are known; body use
owners are updated together with the corresponding target.

```ts : <<annotation header acceptance>>=
            if (header === 'NOTE') {
                annotation = { target: `annotation-note-${encode(document.path)}-${at.line}-${at.column}`,
                    number: result.notes.length + 1, at, tokenIndex, body };
                result.notes.push(annotation);
            } else {
                const match = /^TERM\s+([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{0,99})\s+("(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*")$/.exec(header);
                let title: unknown;
                try { title = match ? JSON.parse(match[2]) : undefined; } catch { title = undefined; }
                if (!match || typeof title !== 'string' || [...title].length > 200) {
                    report(at, 'annotation-header', 'Expected TERM key "title" (JSON string, at most 200 code points) or NOTE');
                    return;
                }
                const base = `annotation-term-${encode(match[1])}`;
                annotation = { key: match[1], title, target: `${base}-${encode(document.path)}-${at.line}-${at.column}`,
                    at, tokenIndex, body };
                result.terms.push(annotation);
            }
```

The temporary location-qualified term targets distinguish duplicate body owners.
Unique terms shed that suffix, giving references an identity stable across prose
edits and document moves. Duplicate definitions remain individually addressable,
but never become a successful key resolution.

```ts : <<annotation final resolution>>=
    const byKey = new Map<string, PublicationTerm[]>();
    for (const term of result.terms) { byKey.set(term.key, [...(byKey.get(term.key) || []), term]); }
    for (const term of result.terms) {
        if (byKey.get(term.key)!.length > 1) {
            report(term.at, 'annotation-duplicate', `Duplicate TERM key: ${term.key}`);
        } else {
            const target = `annotation-term-${encode(term.key)}`;
            for (const use of result.uses) { if (use.owner === term.target) { use.owner = target; } }
            term.target = target;
        }
    }
    for (const use of result.uses) {
        const terms = keyPattern.test(use.key) ? byKey.get(use.key) || [] : [];
        use.resolution = terms.length === 1 ? 'resolved' : terms.length > 1 ? 'ambiguous' : 'missing';
        if (use.resolution === 'resolved') { use.target = terms[0].target; }
        else { report(use.at, `annotation-${use.resolution}`, `${use.resolution} TERM use: ${use.key}`); }
    }
```
